{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"The E–A–B progression transposed down a whole step becomes **D – G – A**.\r\n\r\n**Why this works**  \r\n- Moving every chord down two semitones (a whole step) lowers the entire key from E major to D major, which reduces the vocal range by the same amount.  \r\n- The functional relationships stay the same: D is the I chord, G the IV, and A the V in the new key, so the harmonic feel is unchanged.\r\n\r\n**Guitar shapes**\r\n\r\n| Chord | Fingering (open) | VexTab |\r\n|-------|----------------|--------|\r\n| D major | xx0232 | ```vextab\\ntabstave\\nnotes :w (2/4.2/3.2/2.1/2/0)\\n``` |\r\n| G major | 320003 | ```vextab\\ntabstave\\nnotes :w (3/6.2/5.0/4.0/3/3)\\n``` |\r\n| A major | x02220 | ```vextab\\ntabstave\\nnotes :w (0/5.0/4.2/3.2/2/2)\\n``` |\r\n\r\n**Alternative with capo**  \r\nIf you prefer to keep the familiar E‑shape fingerings, place a capo on the 2nd fret and play the original E‑A‑B shapes; the capo raises the pitch back to D‑G‑A while letting you use the same chord forms. This also keeps voicings and transitions identical to the original progression.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":1970,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":1,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":13,"id":"f-1","kind":null,"label":"skill.transpose","match":null,"prompt":"song is E A B but i cant sing that high, need it in D","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":1}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"Yes, the chords E (E‑G♯‑B) and C (C‑E‑G) share the note E.  \r\n\r\nE major contains the root E, a major third G♯, and a perfect fifth B.  \r\nC major contains the root C, a major third E, and a perfect fifth G.  \r\n\r\nBecause the third of C major is the same pitch as the root of E major, both chords include the note E, even though the other notes differ. This common tone is often used in voice leading or chord progressions to create smooth transitions between the two chords.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":1417,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":3,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":12,"id":"f-12","kind":null,"label":"skill.commontones","match":null,"prompt":"do E and C have any notes in common at all","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"F","verified":2}
{"agent":"theory","answer":"The interval from G up to E (ascending, within the same octave) is a major sixth. This is determined by counting the letter names—G‑A‑B‑C‑D‑E gives six degrees, and by counting semitones—G to E spans nine half‑steps, which defines a major sixth in equal temperament.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":5933,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":1,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":13,"id":"f-20","kind":null,"label":"skill.interval","match":null,"prompt":"from G up to E what do you call that distance","routing":"llm","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":1}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"The diatonic chords in D major are D, Em, F♯m, G, A, Bm, and C♯dim.  \r\n\r\n**Why they work:**  \r\n- **I – D major (D F♯ A)** is the tonal centre.  \r\n- **ii – Em (E G B)** and **iii – F♯m (F♯ A C♯)** provide smooth stepwise motion and common‑tone bridges.  \r\n- **IV – G major (G B D)** and **V – A major (A C♯ E)** give the classic subdominant‑dominant tension that resolves back to I.  \r\n- **vi – Bm (B D F♯)** is the relative minor, useful for darker passages or as a pivot to the parallel minor.  \r\n- **vii° – C♯dim (C♯ E G)** functions as a leading‑tone chord that strongly resolves to D.\r\n\r\n**Common open‑chord voicings (playable):**\r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (2/5.3/4.2/3.1/2.0/1)   // D major (xx0232)\r\n```\r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (0/5.0/4.2/3.2.0/2.0/1)   // Em (022000)\r\n```\r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (2/5.4/4.4/3.2.2/2.2/1)   // F#m (244222)\r\n```\r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (3/5.3/4.0/3.0/2.1/1)   // G major (320003)\r\n```\r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (0/5.0/4.2/3.2.2/2.2/1)   // A major (x02220)\r\n```\r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (2/5.2/4.0/3.0/2.3/1)   // Bm (x24432) – barre shape\r\n```\r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (1/5.1/4.0/3.0/2.0/1)   // C#dim (x4535x) – simple dim shape\r\n```\r\n\r\n**How to use them:**  \r\n- A typical I‑IV‑V progression in D is D → G → A, often with the A resolved as A7 (add the 9th on the high‑E string: 0/5.0/4.2/3.0/2.0/1).  \r\n- For a minor‑feel, move to Bm (vi) or Em (ii).  \r\n- The leading‑tone C♯dim can replace A7 for a stronger pull back to D.  \r\n- Secondary dominants like E7 (V of vi) or B7 (V of iii) expand the palette while still fitting the D‑major key.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":2630,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":14,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":20,"id":"f-28","kind":null,"label":"skill.diatonicchords","match":null,"prompt":"chords that work in D major?","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":14}
