{"agent":"skill.progressioncompletion","answer":"Transposed up a whole step, Am – F – C – G becomes Bm – G – D – A. This moves each chord up two semitones, keeping the original interval pattern and placing the progression in the key of B minor (relative to D major).","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":2727,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":1,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":8,"id":"f-73","kind":null,"label":"skill.transpose","match":null,"prompt":"put Am F C G up a whole step pls","routing":"orchestrator-skill-semantic","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":1}
{"agent":"theory","answer":"C up to Ab is a minor sixth, not an augmented fifth; the interval name follows the letter names, and although C–Ab sounds the same as C–G♯ (an augmented fifth), the spelling determines the correct label. The letters C‑A span six letter steps, making it a sixth. A natural would be a major sixth (9 semitones); lowering the A to Ab reduces it to 8 semitones, which is a minor sixth. The enharmonic equivalent C–G♯ is an augmented fifth, but because the note is written as Ab in the context, we call the interval a minor sixth. This convention is standard in tonal theory and is emphasized in texts such as Aldwell & Schachter.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":22559,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":3,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":3,"id":"f-75","kind":null,"label":"skill.interval","match":null,"prompt":"C up to Ab, is that a minor 6th or an aug 5th, how do u tell","routing":"llm","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"F","verified":2}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"The chord is a **D‑augmented (D+) chord**, functioning as a **secondary dominant (V/V) that resolves to the G‑major (V) chord**.\r\n\r\nA D‑augmented triad consists of the root D, a minor third F, and an augmented fifth A♯ (enharmonically B♭). In tonal harmony the augmented fifth creates a strong pull upward to the tonic of the dominant key—here G. Thus the D+ acts as the dominant of the dominant (V of V), preparing the ear for the upcoming G‑major chord and strengthening the progression toward the tonic. This usage is common in classical and popular music to add tension before resolving to the true dominant.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":2406,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":1,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":137,"id":"m-37","kind":null,"label":"ambiguous","match":null,"prompt":"What is the name and function of the D-F-A♯ chord in this guitar score?","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"music-se","source_author":"Tikhon","source_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source_link":"https://music.stackexchange.com/questions/144196/what-is-the-name-and-function-of-the-d-f-a%e2%99%af-chord-in-this-guitar-score","unchecked":[],"verdict":"F","verified":0}
